meta { title = "Syntax and values", description = "Read native source, local bindings and expression results.", order = 1, group = "Language reference" }

Native source files use the .k extension. Indentation defines suites; use spaces consistently. A colon introduces a function or control-flow body. The final expression supplies the result.

h2 { "Bindings and results" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> int:
  width = 6
  height: int = 7
  area = width * height
  area
""" }

In the released language, bindings are immutable. The unreleased language branch permits numeric and Bool reassignment as described below. An annotation such as height: int declares the expected type; otherwise the checker infers a type. A bare whole-number literal normally infers int, while a decimal or exponent literal normally infers float. An expected type can give a literal a narrower numeric type.

h2 { "Comments and multiline expressions" }

Use # for line comments. Blank lines and comment-only lines do not change block structure. Parentheses and collection delimiters permit multiline expressions:

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn main() -> int:
  # The final expression is the result.
  total = (
    20 +
    22
  )
  total
""" }

Identifiers currently use ASCII letters and underscores, followed by letters, digits or underscores. String literals and comments can contain Unicode. Tabs outside comments and strings are diagnosed.

h2 { "Calls and pipelines" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn add(left: int, right: int) -> int:
  left + right

fn main() -> int:
  40 -> add(2)
""" }

The pipeline inserts its left value as the first argument. Here the final line is equivalent to add(40, 2). Use parentheses when a larger expression needs to be one argument.

h2 { "Reassign a local scalar" }

Unreleased language branch: reuse = to update a numeric or Bool binding while preserving its type. A scalar function parameter is a private value; rebinding it cannot change the caller's binding. Existing scalar names are reassigned across statement branches, while newly introduced names remain scoped to their body. Synchronous, non-fallible CPU functions also support replacing numeric array names as described in the array tutorial. Other array element categories, records, function values and tensors are not reassignable in this stage.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn double(value: int) -> int:
  value = value * 2
  value

fn main() -> int:
  amount = 10
  amount = double(amount)
  amount
""" }

h2 { "Automatic lifetimes" }

Values use automatic lifetime management. User code does not free arrays, tensors, Strings or captured environments. Immutable values may be retained across calls and task boundaries; this is not a promise that every operation avoids a copy.

